Based on your existing provider.tf and the main.tf you showed, you can keep everything in /home/bob/terraform and add the EC2 instance and CloudWatch alarm to the existing main.tf.

One important point: your SNS topic is already represented in main.tf as a Terraform resource, so we'll keep it and reference its ARN from the alarm.

1. Update main.tf

Replace/update /home/bob/terraform/main.tf with:

resource "aws_sns_topic" "sns_topic" {
  name = "devops-sns-topic"
}

resource "aws_instance" "devops_ec2" {
  ami           = "ami-0c02fb55956c7d316"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "devops-ec2"
  }
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "devops_alarm" {
  alarm_name          = "devops-alarm"
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanOrEqualToThreshold"
  evaluation_periods  = 1
  metric_name         = "CPUUtilization"
  namespace           = "AWS/EC2"
  period              = 300
  statistic           = "Average"
  threshold           = 90

  alarm_description = "Alarm when EC2 CPU utilization is >= 90% for 5 minutes"

  dimensions = {
    InstanceId = aws_instance.devops_ec2.id
  }

  alarm_actions = [
    aws_sns_topic.sns_topic.arn
  ]
}
2. Create outputs.tf

Create:

/home/bob/terraform/outputs.tf

with:

output "KKE_instance_name" {
  value = aws_instance.devops_ec2.tags["Name"]
}

output "KKE_alarm_name" {
  value = aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.devops_alarm.alarm_name
}
3. What the CloudWatch settings mean

The important alarm configuration is:

Setting	Value
Alarm name	devops-alarm
Metric	CPUUtilization
Namespace	AWS/EC2
Statistic	Average
Threshold	90
Comparison	>= 90%
Period	300 seconds = 5 minutes
Evaluation periods	1
Instance monitored	devops-ec2
Notification	devops-sns-topic

So the alarm means:

If the average CPU utilization of devops-ec2 is 90% or higher during one 5-minute period, enter the ALARM state and publish a notification to the SNS topic.

